Since $PAD$ is an equilateral triangle with side length 6, the height of $PAD$ is also 6 (by Pythagorean theorem), and the base angle of the pyramid is $30^\circ$ (byতLabeling angles of a regular hexagon).

The surface area of the pyramid is given by the formula $SA=(3/2) Bh$ where $B$ is the area of the base, and $h$ is the height of the pyramid. The base of the pyramid is the regular hexagon $ABCDEF$, and since it is a rhombus, its diagonals bisect each other at the center. Thus, the height of the pyramid is equal to the radius of the circle inscribed in the hexagon, which can be found by dividing the side length 6 by $\sqrt{3}$ (using Thales' theorem).

The angle between the sides of the regular hexagon is $120^\circ$, and the hexagon has 6 edges. Thus, the area of the base of the pyramid is $B=3 \cdot 6^2 \cdot (1-\cos 120^\circ)$ because it is a sector of a circle. There are $6$ sectors of this type in the hexagon, each sharing a common central angle. The total area of the sectors is $6 \times B$, and subtracting the area of the central hexagon gives the area of the pyramid, $A_{pyramid}=6B-1.5B = 4.5B$.

Using the given formula and calculating the area of the pyramid, we get:

\begin{align}
SA & = (3/2)Bh \\
6(4.5A_{pyramid}-1.5B) & = (3/2)(4.5B) (6) \implies A_{pyramid} = 5.4B \\
& = 5.4 \cdot 3 \cdot 6^2 \cdot (1-\cos 120^\circ) \\
& = \boxed{108\sqrt{3}(2-\sqrt{3})} \text{ units }
\end{align}

The pyramid has $\frac{1}{3}$ of the surface area of the regular hexprism with the same base and height, so the edge length is

\begin{align}
\frac{SA}{6} = \frac{108\sqrt{3}(2-\sqrt{3})}{6} &= \boxed{36\sqrt{3}(2-\sqrt{3})} \\
& \approx 36\sqrt{3} \cdot 0.4142 \approx 32.86  \text{ units }
\end{align}

