The possible rational roots of the polynomial are the factors of $16,$ which are $\pm 1, \pm 2, \pm 4.$ Let's compute the value of the polynomial for each possible rational root.

1. Testing for $r=-1$:
\begin{aligned}
(-1)^4+k(-1)^3+(-1)^2+4k(-1)+16&= 1+k-1+4k+16\\
&= 4+4k
\end{aligned}

2. Testing for $r=1$:
\begin{aligned}
(1)^4+k(1)^3+(1)^2+4k(1)+16&= 1+k+1+4k+16\\
&= 20+4k
\end{aligned}

3. Testing for $r=2$:
\begin{aligned}
(2)^4+k(2)^3+(2)^2+4k(2)+16&=16+8k+4+8k+16\\
&=40+16k
\end{aligned}

4. Testing for $r=4$:
\begin{aligned}
(4)^4+k(4)^3+(4)^2+4k(4)+16&=256+64k+16+16k+16\\
&=288+80k
\end{aligned}

None of the rational roots are solutions, which implies that the polynomial has no rational root. This means that the roots are irrational or complex.

