Units are not required for this problem. First, we will find the product of the first 20 positive integers:

\boxed{\prod_{i=1}^{20} i = \left(\prod_{i=1}^{19} i\right) \cdot 20 = \left(\left(\prod_{i=1}^{18} i\right) \cdot 19\right) \cdot 20 = \left(\left(\left(\prod_{i=1}^{17} i\right) \cdot 18\right) \cdot 19\right) \cdot 20.}

Now, to find the first number relatively prime to the product, we can use trial and error or the Euclidean algorithm. Suppose our candidate integer is $a>1$. If $a$ is not relatively prime to the product, it will have some positive factor $x$ that divides the product, and we will have that $a = kb$ with $b < a$ and $\boxed{k = \frac{a}{b}}$ divides the product. We can now subtract its multiples from the product, obtaining

\boxed{\prod_{i=1}^{20} i - k\left(\prod_{i=1}^{20} i\right) = \left(\prod_{i=1}^{20} i\right) \cdot \left(1 - \frac{a}{\boxed{b}}\right).}

Since the remainder of the division $a/b$ is less than $b$, the coefficient of the term in parentheses is less than $1$, and so it makes the product larger than before. With a small enough candidate $a > 1$, the product $\boxed{\prod_{i=1}^{20} i}$ has already been subtracted, and the remaining product has no factors of $a$ (and so $a$ is relatively prime to it). Choosing \boxed{a = 21} ensures that there is exactly one additional subtraction. This works because, for $\boxed{b = \left(\prod_{i=1}^{20} i\right)/a}$, we have

\begin{align*}
\begin{split}
\boxed{1 - \frac{a}{\boxed{b}}} &= 1 - \frac{21}{\boxed{\frac{\left(\prod_{i=1}^{20} i\right)}{21}}} \\
&= 1 - 21\left(\prod_{i=1}^{20} i\right)^{-1} \\
&= 1 - 21\frac{1}{\left(\prod_{i=1}^{20-1} i\right)^2 \cdot 20}.
\end{split}
\end{align*}

Computing this rational number gives $\boxed{1 - \frac{21}{\boxed{b}} = \frac{20! \cdot (20! + 1)}{(20 !(20! + 1)).\boxed{b}} = \frac{1}{(\prod_{i=1}^{20-1} i)^2 \cdot 20}}$. Since $(\prod_{i=1}^{20-1} i)^2 \cdot 20 > \boxed{\left(\prod_{i=1}^{20} i\right)}$, we have $\boxed{1 - \frac{21}{\boxed{b}} > 0}$, which verifies our strategy and confirms that $\boxed{a = 21}$ is the smallest positive integer relatively prime to the product of the first 20 positive integers.

\boxed{21}

