Here's the step-by-step solution:
First, let's find the total number of ways to select any three members out of the 20. This is a combination problem.

\boxed{C(20,3) = \frac{20!}{3!(20-3)!}}

Next, since no member can hold more than one office, we need to find the number of ways to select one president out of the 20 members, one vice-president out of the remaining 19 members, and one treasurer out of the remaining 18 members:

President: \boxed{C(20,1) = \frac{20!}{1! \cdot 19!}}
Vice-president: \boxed{C(19,1) = \frac{19!}{1! \cdot 18!}}
Treasurer: \boxed{C(18,1) = \frac{18!}{1! \cdot 17!}}

Now, to find the total number of valid ways to select a president, vice-president, and treasurer, we multiply the number of ways to select each office individually:

\boxed{\frac{20!}{1! \cdot 19!} \cdot \frac{19!}{1! \cdot 18!} \cdot \frac{18!}{1! \cdot 17!}}

To simplify this expression, we can first factor out the factorials of the common terms and then cancel them out:

\boxed{\frac{20!}{1! \cdot 19!} \cdot \frac{19!}{1! \cdot 18!} \cdot \frac{18!}{1! \cdot 17!} = \frac{20!}{(1!)^3 \cdot 17!}}

Further simplification:
\boxed{\frac{20!\cdot 17!}{(1!)^3 \cdot 17!} = \frac{20!}{(1!)^3}}

Since the 17! factor cancels out, we finally have:

\boxed{\frac{20!}{(1!)^3} = C(20,3)}

Since C(20,3) represents the total number of ways to select any three members out of the 20, and we have already showed that this is also the number of ways to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer with no member holding more than one office, the final answer is:

\boxed{C(20,3)} \text{ ways}

