We have $(2^3)^{(2^3)} = 2^N$. Let's first solve for $N$ using the properties of exponents.
\begin{align*}
(2^3)^{(2^3)} &= 2^N \\
2^{3\times 2^3} &= 2^N \\
2^{3\times 8} &= 2^N
\end{align*}
Since any power of 2 multiplied by itself will result in the same power of 2, we can find the common base:
\begin{align*}
2^{3\times 8} &= 2^N \\
2^{3\times 8} &= 2^{N-3\times 8}
\end{align*}
Now we equate the exponents:
\begin{align*}
3\times 8 &= N-3\times 8 \\
24 &= N-24 \\
N &= 48
\end{align*}
So, the final answer is: \boxed{48}

