Solution:
- We are given the inverse function $h(x)$ and its values for $x=1,2,10$. We want to find $f(f(10))$.
- Since $h(x)=f^{-1}(x)$, we can find the original function $f(x)$ by applying the inverse function to both sides: $x=f(h(x))$.
- Now we can solve for $f(10)$ by rearranging the equation:
\begin{align*}
10 &= f(h(10)) \\
10 &= f(1) \\
\text{ applying } h(1) &= 2, f(2) &= 10 \\
\end{align*}
Finally, we want to find $f(f(10))$. But since we know that $f(2)=10$, we have
\begin{align*}
 f(f(10)) &= f(10) \\
\text{ ( Since } f(2) &= 10 \text{, } f \text{ is one-to-one) } \\
\boxed{10}
\end{align*}

Since there are no units given in the problem, no units should appear in the final answer. But for completeness, you could add " (no units)" after the final solution: \boxed{10} (no units).

