The volume of a truncated right circular cone is given by the formula:

\begin{align*}
V &= \frac{\piʰ² r₂ + \piʰ r₁^2 + \pi r₁ r₂ √(h^2 + r₁^2)}{3} \\
\end{align*}

where $r₁$ is the larger base radius, $r₂$ is the smaller base radius, $h$ is the height, and $π$ and î are the mathematical constants for the circle and the tau number respectively.

In our problem, we are given $r₁ = 8$ cm, $r₂ = 4$ cm, and $h = 6$ cm. Substituting these values in the formula:

\begin{align*}
V &= \frac{\pi (8)^2 + \pi (4)^2 + \pi (8)(4) √(6^2 + 8^2)}{3} \\[5pt]
&= \frac{\pi (64 + 16 + \sqrt{144 + 64})}{3} \\[5pt]
&= \frac{\pi (82 + \sqrt{208})}{3} \\[5pt]
&\approx 32.704 \text{ cubic } cm. \\
\end{align*}

Since we know $V = n \pi$ and for an integer $n$, the volume is in cubic cubic cm $(\text{cm}^3)$, hence we can get the volume of the truncated cone. The integer $n$ must be an integer solution to our given volume of the solid.


