\end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}

To project onto the $y$-axis, we find the vector $\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of the $y$-axis and the orthogonal projection matrix. Let $\mathbf{v}$ be a generic vector in 3D space:
\begin{align*} \mathbf{v} &=
\begin{pmatrix}
x_1\\
y_1\\
z_1
\end{pmatrix}
\\
\mathbf{u} &=
\begin{pmatrix}
0\\
1\\
0
\end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}

The orthogonal projection of $\mathbf{v}$ on $\mathbf{u}$, denoted as $\mathbf{v}'$, can be computed as:
\begin{align*} \mathbf{v}' &= \mathbf{u} \left( \mathbf{u}^T \mathbf{v} \right) = \begin{pmatrix} 0\\1\\0 \end{pmatrix} \left( \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x_1\\ y_1\\ z_1 \end{pmatrix} \right) = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ y_1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} \end{align*}

To find the matrix that corresponds to the projection onto the $y$-axis, we consider an arbitrary vector $\mathbf{x}$:
