First, let's represent a general vector: \vec{x} = (x_1, x_2). We want to project this vector onto the $y$-axis, which means we are interested in the $y$-coordinate. The projection of $\vec{x}$ onto the $y$-axis is therefore:

\begin{align*}
\text{Projection}(\vec{x}) &= \left(\begin{array}{c} 0 \\ \end{array}\right) + t(1, 0) \\
&= (0, 0) + t(1, 0) \\
&= (t, 0)
\end{align*}

The original vector $\vec{x}$ can be projected onto this line, which gives us a vector representing the projection. The direction vector of the line containing the projection is (1, 0). The matrix representing this projection is:

\begin{align*}
\text{Matrix projection} &= \left(\begin{array}{c} (1, 0) \\ \end{array}\right) \left(\begin{array}{cc} \cos \theta & -\sin \theta \\ \sin \theta & \cos \theta \\ \end{array}\right)
\end{align*}

In this case, recall that the dot product between $\vec{x}$ and the direction vector of the projection line's unit vector is equal to $|\vec{x}||\text{direction}|\cos \theta$:

\begin{align*}
\vec{x} \cdot \text{direction vector} &= |\vec{x}||\text{direction}|\cos \theta \\
(x_1, x_2)\left(\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ 0 \end{array}\right) &= |(x_1, x_2)|\cdot1\cdot\cos \theta \\
x_1 &= |(x_1, x_2)|\cdot\cos \theta \\
\text{Since }\vec{x} &= (x_1, x_2) \text{, then } |(x_1, x_2)| = \sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2} \\
\text{So, } \cos \theta &= \frac{x_1}{\sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2}} \\
\end{align*}

This gives us the cosine of the angle between $\vec{x}$ and the direction vector, which is the direction cosine $\cos \theta$ we were looking for. Now, we can substitute this back into the matrix equation:

\begin{align*}
\text{Matrix projection} &= \left(\begin{array}{c} (1, 0) \\ \end{array}\right) \left(\begin{array}{cc} \cos \theta & -\sin \theta \\ \sin \theta & \cos \theta \\ \end{array}\right) \\
&= \left(\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ 0 \end{array}\right) \left( \left(\frac{x_1}{\sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2}}\right) \left(\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ 0 \end{array}\right)
- \left(\frac{x_2}{\sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2}}\right) \left(\begin{array}{c} 0 \\ 1 \end{array}\right) \right) \\
&= \left(\begin{array}{c} \frac{x_1^2}{x_1^2 + x_2^2} \\ 0 \end{array}\right)
\end{align*}

Simplifying this expression, we have:

\begin{align*}
\text{Matrix projection} &= \left(\begin{array}{c} \frac{x_1^2}{x_1^2 + x_2^2} \\ 0 \end{array}\right) \\
&= \left(\begin{array}{c} \frac{x_2^2}{x_1^2 + x_2^2} \\ 0 \end{array}\right) \text{ (since } x_1^2 = x_2^2 \text{ due to projection onto the } y\text{-axis)} \\
\boxed{ \left(\begin{array}{c} \frac{x_2^2}{x_1^2 + x_2^2} \\ 0 \end{array}\right) }

