First, we will find a vector that spans the y-axis. A general vector can be represented as $v = (x, y)$. To be on the y-axis, $x = 0$. So the vector that we want to project onto the y-axis must have a zero for its x-component and a non-zero value for its y-component. For example, let's pick the unit vector: $v = (0, 1)$.

Next, we find the orthogonal projection of an arbitrary vector $w = (x, y)$ onto $v$ by finding the vector $p$ such that $p \cdot v = w \cdot v$ and $p \cdot u = 0$, where $u$ is any vector that is orthogonal to $v$. In this case, $u$ is the vector $(1, 0)$.

The dot product $w \cdot v$ is $(x, y) \cdot (0, 1) = y$, and the dot product $w \cdot u$ is $(x, y) \cdot (1, 0) = x$. Since we want $p$ to be orthogonal to $u$, we have $x = 0$. So, we need to find $y$ such that $y\cdot 1 = y = w \cdot v$. This gives us $y = w \cdot v$.

Next, we form the matrix $P$ corresponding to the projection by finding a matrix multiplication that produces the orthogonal projection of $w$ onto $v$. First, we form the matrix $W$ whose columns are the components of $w$:

\begin{equation*}
W = \left[
    \begin{array}{cc}
        0 & 1 \\
        y & w \cdot v
    \end{array}
   \right]
\end{equation*}

The matrix $P$ is the orthogonal projection of $W$ onto $v$:

\begin{equation*}
P = (v \cdot W^\intercal ) (v \cdot v^\intercal )^{-1}
\end{equation*}

But $v \cdot v^\intercal = (1, 0)
\cdot
[
    0 & 1 \\
    0 & 0
] = 0 + 0 = 0$, since the dot product of a vector with itself is zero if it contains a zero component. To avoid division by zero, we need to complete the square such that the square of the vector $v$ is a rank one matrix:

\begin{equation*}
P = (v \cdot W^\intercal ) \left( [v \cdot v^\intercal , v] [v \cdot v^\intercal , v]^\intercal \right)^{-1}
\end{equation*}

Using the matrix specified inverses formula:

\begin{equation*}
P = (v \cdot W^\intercal ) \left( [ [v \cdot v^\intercal , v] [v \cdot v^\intercal , v]^\intercal ]^{-1} [v \cdot v^\intercal , v] \right)
\end{equation*}

Now we can compute the necessary matrices:

\begin{equation*}
[v \cdot v^\intercal , v] = [ ... ] = \boxed{\left[
    \begin{array}{cc}
        0 & 0\\
        0 & 1
    \end{array}
\right]}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
[v \cdot v^\intercal , v] [v \cdot v^\intercal , v]^\intercal
= \boxed{\left[
