To solve this problem, we can use some basic properties of exponents. First, we can extract the hundreds digit of the power - this is the same as considering the units digit of the base raised to the power:
\begin{equation}
\text{units digit of } 2011^{2011} = \text{units digit of } 1^{2011} \text{ (because } 2011 \equiv 1 (\mod 10) \text{)}
\end{equation}
The units digit of 1 raised to any power is always 1:
\begin{equation}
\text{units digit of } 1^{2011} = 1
\end{equation}
However, our goal is to find the hundreds digit, which is three places to the left of the units digit. Since the units digit is 1 and the hundreds digit is 0 in base-10, we multiply the units digit by 10 raised to the power of the number of places we want to move left:
\begin{equation}
\text{hundreds digit of } 2011^{2011} = \text{units digit of } 1^{2011} \times 10^{3}
\end{equation}
Multiply the two terms:
\begin{equation}
1 \times 10^{3} = 1000
\end{equation}
But the question asks for the hundreds digit, which is the rightmost three digits of 1000. Therefore, we take the rightmost three digits:
\begin{equation}
\text{hundreds digit of } 2011^{2011} = 100
\end{equation}
At this point, we have the answer in an exact numeric expression, but it may still have unnecessary zeros. To remove those, we can use the plain text to express the units as "hundreds":
\boxed{100 \text{ hundreds }} (final answer)

