To solve this problem, we cannot use numerical methods as we need an exact value without any unnecessary decimals or approximations. Instead, we will exploit the symmetry of the cube root of unity to find the roots of the given equation.

The equation $z^6+z^3+1=0$ has the roots $\{1,i,\omega,\bar{\omega},-\omega,-\bar{\omega}\}$, where $\omega = e^{2\pi i/3}$ is a primitive cube root of unity. We know that $i$ is one of the roots, but we are looking for an argument between $90^\circ$ and $180^\circ$. Thus, we consider the roots that are products between $i$ and $\omega$.

The argument of a complex number $z$ is defined by $\arg(z)=\theta$, where $\theta$ is the angle in the polar representation of $z$ given by $z = r(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta)$.

Since $\omega^2 = -1/2-i\sqrt{3}/2$ and $i^2 = -1$, it follows that $\omega = \sqrt{-1-\omega^2}$, so $\arg(\omega) = -\arctan(\sqrt{3}/2) - 2\pi/3$.

Next, let's consider the product of $i$ and $\omega$:
\begin{align*}
    i\omega &= i(\cos(-\arctan(\sqrt{3}/2) - 2\pi/3)+i\sin(-\arctan(\sqrt{3}/2) - 2\pi/3)) \\
        &= \left(\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)\right)+i\left(\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)\right)\left(-\cos\left(\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right)-\sin\left(\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right)\right) \\
        &= \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}+\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right)+i\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}+\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right)\text{.}
\end{align*}

Now we need to find the argument of $\omega i$, which is the angle in the polar form:
\begin{align*}
    \arg(\omega i) &= \theta\\
        &= \left(\frac{\pi}{6}+\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right)\text{.}
\end{align*}

Since $\arg(z)$ is an angle, it has a range of $360^\circ$ and is periodic with period $360^\circ$. Therefore, we can simply add $360^\circ$ until the argument falls into the range requested:

\begin{align*}
\arg(\omega i) &= \left(\frac{\pi}{6}+\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right) \\
      &= \left(\frac{\pi}{6}+\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right)+ 360^\circ \times 1 \\
      &= \left(\frac{\pi}{6}+\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right)+ 180^\circ \text{.}
\end{align*}

Now we can calculate an exact value for our periodic function:

\begin{align*}
\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}+\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right) & = \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)\cos\left(\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right) - \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)\sin\left(\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right) \\
  &= \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2\sqrt{2}}\text{, }
\end{align*}

and just to smoothly insert the $\sin$ term:
\begin{align*}
   \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}+\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right) & = \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)\cos\left(\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right)+\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)\sin\left(\arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right) \\
  &= \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2\sqrt{2}}\text{.}
\end{align*}

This yields:
\begin{align*}
    \arg(\omega i) & = \arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{\sqrt{3}+1}\right) + \frac{\pi}{3} \text{,}
\end{align*}

where we used the identity $\arctan(x)+\arctan(y)=\arctan\left(\frac{x+y}{1-xy}\right)$.

Now find the arctan value:
\begin{align*}
  \arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{\sqrt{3}+1}\right)
  &= \arctan\left(\sqrt{\frac{3}{4+3}}\right) \\
  &= \arctan\left(\sqrt{\frac{3}{7}}\right) \\
  &= \arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2\sqrt{7}}\right) + \arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2\sqrt{7}}\right) \text{ (since } \arctan(x)+\arctan(x)=90^\circ\text{)} \\
  &= 45^\circ \text{.}
\end{align*}

Thus, putting it all together:
\begin{align*}
\arg(\omega i) &= \arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2\sqrt{7}}\right) + \arctan\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2\sqrt{7}}\right) + \frac{\pi}{3}\\
  &= \frac{135^\circ + 45^\circ}{2}\text{.}
\end{align*}

Since the roots of equation are symmetric about the real axis, another root is the argument of $-\omega i$, which is the negative of our found argument:

\begin{align*}
\arg(-\omega i) &= -\arg(\omega i)\\
