The formula for the volume of a cube is:

\text{ Volume }= \text{ edge length }^3

A 2-cm-by-2cm-by-2cm cube has an initial volume of \boxed{ 8 \text{ cubic cm } }

If the volume is increased by 19 cubic centimeters:

\text{ New Volume } = \text{ initial volume } + 19
\text{ New Volume } = 8\text{ cubic cm } + 19\text{ cubic cm } = 27\text{ cubic cm }

Let's denote the side length of the new cube as X. The new volume can be represented as:

\text{ New Volume } = \text{ edge length(new) }^3 = \boxed{ X^3 = 27 }

By solving this equation, we find:

\boxed{ X \boxed{ = \text{ cube root of }27 } } = \boxed{ X = 3\sqrt[3]{3} \text{ cm } }

completions_1:
The volume of a cube is the cube of its edge length. Let $x$ denote the original length of one edge. The volume of the original cube (in cubic centimeters) is $x^3$ cm³. The original cube's volume increased by $19$ cubic centimeters, so the volume of the new cube is $(x^3) + 19$ cubic centimeters. Since the new cube is also a cube, the volume of the new cube is also equal to the cube of the length of one edge of the new cube: $(y^3) = (x^3) + 19$, where $y$ denotes the length of one edge of the new cube. Rearranging the equation, we get $y^3 - x^3 = 19$. Solving this equation requires more advanced mathematics. One approach is to use the formula for the cube root of a polynomial using Newton's method, but cubic equations cannot be solved exactly using only arithmetic operations and the cube root function. However, one can use a calculator to approximate the solution.

Using a calculator, the solution to $y^3 - x^3 = 19$ can be approximated to three decimal places as $y \approx 2.571$. However, since $x$ is known to be in whole centimeters, the solution must also be in whole centimeters. Therefore, rounding the decimal to the nearest whole centimeter gives $y \approx 3$ cm.

\boxed{ 3 \text{ cm } }

