The volume of a cube is given by $ V = l^3 $, where $ l $ is the length of one edge. If the volume of the original cube is 8 cubic centimeters, we have:

\begin{align*}
8 \text{ cubic centimeters} &= (2 \text{ centimeters})^3\\
8 &= 8 \text {centimeters}^3
\end{align*}

The volume of the new cube is 8 cubic centimeters + 19 cubic centimeters, and the volume of the new cube is still given by $ V = l^3 $. Thus, we have:

\begin{align*}
\boxed{(2 \text{ centimeters})^3 + 19 \text{ cubic centimeters}} &= \boxed{l^3}\\
2^3 + 19 &= \boxed{l^3}\\
18 + 19 &= \boxed{l^3}\\
37 &= \boxed{l^3}
\end{align*}

Since we're looking for the length of one edge of the new cube (l), we can take the cube root of both sides. In LaTex, we have:

\begin{align*}
\boxed{l} &= \boxed{\sqrt[3]{37}}\\
\text{ The length of one edge of the new cube is }\boxed{\sqrt[3]{37}\text{ centimeters}}
\end{align*}

This is the exact solution in terms of the cube root, and it is not easily expressed in simple fractions and a smaller number of roots. The closest approximation for three decimal places is:

\begin{align*}
\boxed{\sqrt[3]{37}} &\approx 3.495 \\
\text{ The length of one edge of the new cube is }\boxed{3.495\text{ centimeters}}
\end{align*}
